WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.989
Left of the line, three.js.

00:00:01.989 --> 00:00:04.759
Right of it, the same scene file, path traced.

00:00:05.139 --> 00:00:06.830
The engine is called ThreeRT.

00:00:09.373 --> 00:00:10.448
Hi, I'm Albedo.

00:00:10.448 --> 00:00:15.106
ThreeRT runs three.js style code and traces its light on ray tracing hardware.

00:00:15.106 --> 00:00:18.331
Here is how, and what its sixty frames cost.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.534
Scene code never imports three.

00:00:21.534 --> 00:00:23.963
It imports a lookalike in physical units.

00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:27.501
Every change becomes a call on a small engine interface.

00:00:27.921 --> 00:00:31.272
In the native app, the script runs in QuickJS, and a Rust

00:00:31.272 --> 00:00:33.227
engine owns the scene and the frame.

00:00:33.647 --> 00:00:36.796
In a browser, the same calls drive plain three.js.

00:00:36.796 --> 00:00:37.846
The game works.

00:00:37.846 --> 00:00:39.595
The traced light stays behind.

00:00:41.283 --> 00:00:43.977
A live frame starts with one sample per pixel.

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:45.774
The camera ray finds the floor.

00:00:46.194 --> 00:00:49.434
There, the kernel samples the lamp directly, with a shadow ray.

00:00:49.994 --> 00:00:53.536
Then the path bounces once and stops, reading the light from a

00:00:53.536 --> 00:00:55.307
cache voxel that other samples trained.

00:00:55.727 --> 00:00:59.561
Every pixel, every frame, while one tile in eight trains the cache.

00:00:59.561 --> 00:01:00.839
Less noise, some bias.

00:01:02.558 --> 00:01:04.294
The raw trace is noisy and small.

00:01:05.158 --> 00:01:07.586
It is blended with its reprojected history.

00:01:07.986 --> 00:01:10.989
Up to five wavelet passes smooth what is still noisy.

00:01:11.389 --> 00:01:13.635
An upscaler takes it to full size.

00:01:13.635 --> 00:01:16.201
On Bistro, the trace runs near half resolution.

00:01:16.601 --> 00:01:18.334
Then Unreal's bloom and tone map.

00:01:20.283 --> 00:01:21.956
Every glowing object is a light.

00:01:21.956 --> 00:01:24.466
Knock them over, and the light moves with them.

00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:30.688
The repository's numbers, on an Apple M five.

00:01:30.688 --> 00:01:35.270
Live, fifteen and a half milliseconds at render scale point five five.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:36.416
Full resolution, forty.

00:01:38.158 --> 00:01:43.327
Against its own offline reference, the live frame is close, far closer than three.js.

00:01:43.707 --> 00:01:45.166
Three.js wins on cost.

00:01:45.166 --> 00:01:47.719
Small scenes take it about a millisecond.

00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:51.619
Tokyo, from Japan's open city model.

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:54.120
Seventeen million triangles near the tower.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.733
Afternoon into night, no rebuild, again through the upscaler.

00:01:58.908 --> 00:02:02.687
On a Steam Deck, the game's own counter reads thirty-four frames a second.

00:02:03.067 --> 00:02:06.410
Windows is an unsigned preview, and the clients are closed.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:07.747
The engine is MIT.

00:02:09.225 --> 00:02:13.461
Sixty frames here means one sample, one bounce, a cache and an upscaler.

00:02:13.461 --> 00:02:14.113
It works.

00:02:17.358 --> 00:02:22.184
Three.js shaped code, a path tracer underneath, sixty frames bought with a cache.

00:02:22.604 --> 00:02:24.722
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:24.722 --> 00:02:25.327
I'm Albedo.

00:02:25.327 --> 00:02:25.630
Bye!
